.TH CHTTPCLIENT_DO_ASYNC_STREAMING 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_do_async_streaming \- submit an HTTP request for asynchronous execution with a streaming response body
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpclient.h>

ctpool_future *chttpclient_do_async_streaming(chttpcli cli,
                                               const chttp_request_t *req,
                                               chttpcli_write_fn write_fn,
                                               void *write_ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Same non-blocking submission semantics as chttpclient_do_async, but
write_fn is invoked one or more times with chunks of the response
body as they arrive over the wire, exactly like
chttpclient_do_streaming.

write_fn runs on one of the engine's own reactor threads, NOT on the
calling thread and NOT on a dedicated thread for this request.  This
has two hard requirements, unlike chttpclient_do_streaming's
caller-thread callback: write_fn must not block (no blocking I/O, no
long-held locks, no waiting on another request's future) (doing so
stalls every other connection the engine is currently multiplexing
on that reactor thread) and write_fn must not call back into
chttpclient_do_async/_streaming (or anything that transitively waits
on this same request's future) for the same or a different chttpcli
sharing the engine, or it may deadlock against the very reactor
thread it is running on.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli       Client handle; must not be NULL.
req       Request to execute; must not be NULL.
write_fn  Chunk delivery callback; must not be NULL.
write_ctx Opaque context pointer passed verbatim to write_fn.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A future, or NULL under the same conditions as chttpclient_do_async
(including a NULL write_fn).  On success, the future's eventual
result carries ccol_http_transfer_aborted if write_fn ever returns
fewer bytes than len, aborting the transfer.
.fi
